From b10fddae61b3fe8bf1b67c83ac00fd149f2f6ed6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Max schwenk <maschwenk@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 03:09:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Give tuple references lazy member tables

Tuple references were left out of the lazy member tables. A tuple
reference resolved in full instantiates its element properties and length
and resolves its base type Array<E1 | E2 | ...> with this = the tuple in
full, a new reference with ~40 instantiated members per tuple. Tuple
targets have declared members and base types like interfaces, and every
consumer already goes through the lazy-aware accessors, so the only change
is the condition in getReadyLazyMemberTableWorker. The Array base gets a
lazy table of its own, so only the members that are looked up get
instantiated. (The old ObjectFlagsTuple exclusion was redundant with the
ClassOrInterface test.)

38k-file program (37,942 files), tsgo on main + #64475 + #64526, median
of 3, single threaded / 4 checkers: symbols 15,330,783 -> 13,297,217
(-13.3%) / 22,811,135 -> 19,553,613 (-14.3%), types and instantiations
unchanged, heap after check 11.01 -> 10.47 GB (-4.9%) / 16.32 -> 15.41 GB
(-5.5%), peak footprint 13.88 -> 13.12 GB single threaded.

Prototype in a Rust port of the checker (same symbol/type/instantiation
counts as this commit on the same program, single threaded and with 4
checkers): symbols -13.3% / -14.3%, peak -4.7% / -5.1%; 66,727 / 100,248
tuple tables, 8,402 / 11,824 (13% / 12%) later resolved in full; declared
members in lazy tables +2.82M single threaded, of which +0.66M are
instantiated, so 77% of the new lazy members are never created.

tupleLazyMembers.ts covers element access, length, array methods through
the this-typed base, optional/rest/named/readonly tuples and weak type and
assignability checks. Baselines are generated on main without #64475 and
#64526.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
